managed         canonical ok, equals the built graph
gp-parse        canonical ok, re-encodes to the canonical form
managed         +80 B     ok, equals the built graph
gp-parse        +80 B     ok, re-encodes to the canonical form

Reading this.
  Both decoders normalise the +80 B form back to the canonical one, because
  an empty map value and an absent map value are the SAME facade value and
  neither encoder writes it. So the two forms round-trip to one another's
  values and this slice's byte-identity gate is unaffected.

  A P2.5 TIMING row therefore carries the manifest's 19,632 B in every arm
  of this slice, and is not comparable with a slice whose encoder writes
  19,712 B: 80 bytes and 40 extra fields are not the same work.

